      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Facial aging is a complex interplay of extrinsic and intrinsic factors leading to progressive changes in the skin, subcutaneous tissue, and bone. Clinical experience suggests that early aesthetic intervention may slow the signs of aging, but treatment in the absence of symptoms or with minimal signs of aging has not yet been properly addressed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To provide treatment recommendations for primary prevention and early intervention in individuals with no or minimal signs of aging."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Fourteen specialists in aesthetic medicine convened over a full-day meeting under the guidance of a certified moderator."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Tailored treatment recommendations have been provided for prevention and early intervention of fine wrinkles, static lines and folds, irregular pigmentation, laxity, and subcutaneous volume loss by protecting the epidermis, stimulating neocollagenesis, reducing hyperkinetic musculature, and reinforcing supporting structures."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Preventive measures and early therapeutic interventions that may alter the course of facial aging were defined. Further studies are needed to support these recommendations with the best possible evidence. <p><em>J Drugs Dermatol. 2017;16(9):846-854.<\/em><\/p>."
        }
      ]},
